\title{Inverse Stereographic Tropical Lift:\\Rigidity, Degree Collapse, and Compactness}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 31, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study a one-dimensional max-plus analogue of stereographic projection. The finite tropical projective line is the quotient of $\R^2$ by diagonal translation and is canonically parametrized by $x=x_1-x_0$. In this coordinate, the proposed pole construction is
\[
S(x)=\max(2x,x)-\max(x,0).
\]
Although this expression has a quadratic-over-linear presentation, it simplifies globally to $S(x)=x$. More generally, moving the finite pole to $p\in\R$ gives
\[
S_p(x)=\max(2x,x+p)-\max(x,p),
\]
and every member of this family is again the identity. Thus pole position disappears after projective normalization. The map is a homeomorphism from the finite tropical projective line to $\R$, but its minimal degree cannot be two because it has a linear presentation. Under the compactified convention, the source is compact; consequently, no homeomorphism to $\R$ exists. We give elementary proofs, evaluation algorithms, numerical diagnostics, and implications for intrinsic tropical rational degree.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

In the max-plus convention, tropical arithmetic is defined by
\[
a\oplus b=\max(a,b),\qquad a\odot b=a+b.
\]
Repeated tropical multiplication turns $x$ into an ordinary integer multiple: the tropical square is $2x$. Tropical polynomials are therefore maxima of affine functions. Their graphs are piecewise linear, and their breakpoints record changes in the dominant monomial.

Classical stereographic projection uses a pole to send a sphere minus that pole to an affine plane. In dimension one, fractional-linear transformations provide the associated coordinate changes. This motivates a tropical question: can a pole construction define a tropical rational homeomorphism from a tropical projective line to a tropical affine line, and does it have a meaningful degree-two character?

Two issues must be separated. First, the tropical projective line may mean either a quotient formed solely from finite coordinates or a compactified object admitting infinite coordinates. These spaces have different topology. Second, the syntactic degree of a difference of tropical polynomials may not survive cancellation. A formula displaying a quadratic term need not define a function of minimal degree two.

We resolve both issues for the proposed one-dimensional map. The finite map is exactly the identity. Moving the pole does not produce a nontrivial family. The map possesses the requested quadratic-over-linear presentation, but also a linear presentation. The compactified source cannot be homeomorphic to the noncompact real line. The analysis isolates three principles: projective normalization may erase parameters; common max-plus factors can cancel; and compactification constrains admissible targets.

\section{Max-plus preliminaries}

\subsection{Tropical polynomials and rational functions}

A one-variable tropical polynomial has the form
\[
P(x)=\max_{0\leq i\leq m}(ix+a_i),
\]
where the coefficients $a_i$ are real. Such a function is convex and piecewise affine. A tropical rational function can be represented as a difference
\[
R(x)=P(x)-Q(x)
\]
of tropical polynomials. It is continuous and piecewise affine, though not necessarily convex.

The same function may have many presentations. The identity
\[
c+\max(u,v)=\max(c+u,c+v)
\]
allows common affine contributions to be factored out. When identical structure occurs in both $P$ and $Q$, subtraction cancels it. Thus the largest displayed exponent in one presentation is not automatically an invariant.

\subsection{Finite tropical projective space}

\begin{definition}[Finite tropical projective line]
Two pairs $(x_0,x_1)$ and $(y_0,y_1)$ are projectively equivalent when there exists $\lambda\in\R$ such that
\[
(y_0,y_1)=(x_0+\lambda,x_1+\lambda).
\]
The quotient is denoted $\TP^1_{\mathrm{fin}}$.
\end{definition}

The difference $\nu(x_0,x_1)=x_1-x_0$ is invariant under simultaneous translation. Every class has the unique representative $(0,x)$.

\begin{proposition}[Normalized coordinate]
The map sending the projective class of $(x_0,x_1)$ to $x_1-x_0$ is a bijection from $\TP^1_{\mathrm{fin}}$ to $\R$. With the normalized-coordinate topology, it is a homeomorphism.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Simultaneous translation leaves the difference unchanged. Every $x\in\R$ is represented by $(0,x)$, proving surjectivity. Equal differences imply that two pairs differ by a diagonal translation, proving injectivity. The topological statement follows from the normalized-coordinate topology.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Compactified convention}

A second convention admits infinite coordinates. In dimension one, the normalized compactified line is modeled by
\[
\overline{\R}=[-\infty,+\infty]
\]
with its order topology; this space is compact.

\begin{definition}[Compactified tropical projective line]
The compactified tropical projective line $\TP^1_{\mathrm{comp}}$ is the endpoint-completed normalized tropical line, identified topologically with $\overline{\R}$.
\end{definition}

This convention is not interchangeable with the finite one in questions of global homeomorphism.

\section{The pole construction}

\subsection{Presentation classes}

The proposed inverse stereographic tropical lift is
\[
S(x)=\max(2x,x)-\max(x,0).
\]
The first maximum contains quadratic and linear tropical monomials, while the second is tropical linear.

\begin{definition}[Pole-shaped quadratic presentation]
A function $f:\R\to\R$ has a pole-shaped quadratic tropical presentation if there exist $a,b,c,d\in\R$ such that
\[
f(x)=\max(2x+a,x+b)-\max(x+c,d)
\]
for every $x\in\R$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Linear presentation]
A function $f:\R\to\R$ has a linear tropical presentation in normalized coordinate if there exists $c\in\R$ such that
\[
f(x)=x+c
\]
for every $x\in\R$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Global simplification}

\begin{theorem}[Identity theorem]
For every $x\in\R$,
\[
S(x)=x.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $x\leq0$, then $2x\leq x$, so $\max(2x,x)=x$ and $\max(x,0)=0$. Hence $S(x)=x$. If $x\geq0$, then $\max(2x,x)=2x$ and $\max(x,0)=x$, so $S(x)=2x-x=x$. The calculations agree at $x=0$.
\end{proof}

The apparent breakpoint in each maximum produces no breakpoint in their difference.

\subsection{Arbitrary finite poles}

Move the pole to $p\in\R$ and define
\[
S_p(x)=\max(2x,x+p)-\max(x,p).
\]

\begin{theorem}[Finite-pole rigidity]
For every $p,x\in\R$,
\[
S_p(x)=x.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Distributivity gives
\[
\max(2x,x+p)=\max(x+x,x+p)=x+\max(x,p).
\]
Consequently,
\[
S_p(x)=x+\max(x,p)-\max(x,p)=x.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Pole independence]
For all finite pole positions $p,q\in\R$, the functions $S_p$ and $S_q$ are equal.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Both functions equal the identity by finite-pole rigidity.
\end{proof}

The pole parameter appears only through a common max-plus factor and is absent from the normalized function.

\section{Topological consequences}

\begin{theorem}[Finite tropical stereographic homeomorphism]
The map induced by
\[
S(x)=\max(2x,x)-\max(x,0)
\]
is a homeomorphism from $\TP^1_{\mathrm{fin}}$ to $\R$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The normalized coordinate identifies $\TP^1_{\mathrm{fin}}$ with $\R$. The identity theorem says that $S$ is the identity in this coordinate. It is bijective, its inverse is the identity, and both directions are continuous.
\end{proof}

This confirms a homeomorphism on the finite model, but not a nontrivial pole-dependent analogue of classical stereography.

\begin{theorem}[No compactified-to-affine homeomorphism]
There is no homeomorphism
\[
\TP^1_{\mathrm{comp}}\longrightarrow\R.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The source is compact. The continuous image of a compact space is compact. A homeomorphism onto $\R$ would therefore force $\R$ to be compact, contrary to its noncompactness.
\end{proof}

The natural target for compactified stereography is an extended real line or another compact space.

\section{Presentation degree and cancellation}

\begin{proposition}[Quadratic presentation]
The function $S$ has a pole-shaped quadratic tropical presentation.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Choose $a=b=c=d=0$ in the definition. The resulting expression is precisely $S(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Linear representability]
The same function $S$ has a linear presentation.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The identity theorem gives $S(x)=x=x+0$ for every $x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Failure of minimal degree two]
It is false that $S$ is pole-shaped quadratic-presentable but not linearly presentable. In particular, the displayed quadratic term does not imply that the represented function has minimal tropical rational degree exactly two.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The preceding propositions provide both a quadratic-over-linear presentation and a linear presentation. Thus the asserted absence of a linear presentation is false.
\end{proof}

For the pole family, put
\[
P_p(x)=\max(2x,x+p),\qquad Q_p(x)=\max(x,p).
\]
Then $P_p(x)=x+Q_p(x)$. The numerator and denominator each bend at $x=p$, but their difference has no bend. A representation-independent degree should assign equal functions equal degree, classify translations as degree at most one, ignore common tropical factors, and disregard breakpoints cancelled in the difference.

\section{Algorithms and numerical diagnostics}

\subsection{Direct evaluation}

The map is evaluated by two comparisons and constant-time arithmetic:
\begin{verbatim}
INPUT: real number x
numerator   <- max(2*x, x)
denominator <- max(x, 0)
RETURN numerator - denominator
\end{verbatim}
It uses $O(1)$ time and $O(1)$ memory per input. A diagnostic table is
\[
\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
 x&-3&-1&0&2&5\\ \hline
 S(x)&-3&-1&0&2&5
\end{array}
\]
which samples both branches and the breakpoint.

\subsection{Pole-family evaluation}

For a finite pole $p$, evaluate
\begin{verbatim}
INPUT: real pole p, real coordinate x
numerator   <- max(2*x, x+p)
denominator <- max(x, p)
RETURN numerator - denominator
\end{verbatim}
This also uses $O(1)$ time and memory. On a grid of $n$ inputs and $m$ poles, exhaustive evaluation takes $O(nm)$ time and $O(1)$ auxiliary memory if streamed.

\subsection{Branch audit}

If $x\leq p$, then
\[
\max(2x,x+p)=x+p,\qquad \max(x,p)=p,
\]
so the difference is $x$. If $x\geq p$, then
\[
\max(2x,x+p)=2x,\qquad \max(x,p)=x,
\]
and the difference is again $x$. Both components switch branch at the same point. This synchronized switch is the numerical signature of cancellation.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

\subsection{Tropical coordinate design}

The finite quotient removes diagonal translation. A construction depending on absolute representatives must therefore be checked after normalization. The pole-family theorem shows that a parameter can be gauge-dependent: moving $p$ changes unreduced components but not their difference. Tropical moduli constructions may need marked ends, a metric normalization, or additional data to preserve such a parameter.

\subsection{Piecewise-linear model complexity}

Differences of maxima occur in optimization and piecewise-linear modeling. A max-affine component may have several regions even when the final difference is globally affine. Here both $P_p$ and $Q_p$ have a breakpoint, while $P_p-Q_p$ has none. Simplification and model-compression algorithms should detect and cancel shared breakpoints.

\subsection{Tropical M\"obius transformations}

A one-dimensional orientation-preserving affine map of slope one is $x\mapsto x+c$. The present map is the case $c=0$. More generally, one may ask which functions
\[
R(x)=\max_i(a_i x+b_i)-\max_j(c_j x+d_j)
\]
are homeomorphisms of $\R$. Necessary conditions include continuity, strict monotonicity, and unbounded limits of opposite sign at the ends. Their slopes on every linearity interval must be positive. The cancellation found here motivates asking whether every reduced slope-one homeomorphism is a translation.

\subsection{Compactified targets}

The compactness obstruction is a target-selection principle. If endpoints occur in the source, they must be represented in the codomain. A compactified map should take values in $\overline{\R}$ and state explicitly where the ends map. Reusing an affine target loses the global topology.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Comparison with classical stereography}

Classical stereographic projection supplies a global coordinate away from a pole, depends on the pole, and carries conformal information. The present max-plus construction retains only the first feature: it is the normalized coordinate itself. Pole dependence disappears by common-factor cancellation, while no assertion of angle preservation is available in a one-dimensional piecewise-linear setting. The term ``stereographic'' records the origin of the formula, not a claim that every classical property survives tropicalization. A richer higher-dimensional analogue may require metric or harmonic conditions in addition to tropical rationality.

\subsection{Stability and exactness}

The identity is algebraic rather than approximate. Nevertheless, direct floating-point evaluation of two large, nearly equal maxima can suffer subtractive cancellation: intermediate quantities may be much larger than the output. A robust implementation can return $x$ after recognizing the shared factor instead of evaluating the unreduced expression. Symbolic reduction thus lowers the operation count, removes a branch, and avoids unnecessary loss of precision. The unreduced form remains useful for auditing component geometry, while the reduced form is preferable for production computation.

\subsection{Scope of the compactness argument}

The topological obstruction uses no special algebraic property of the pole formula. It excludes every possible homeomorphism from the compactified source to $\R$, even maps not expressible tropically. It does not obstruct continuous maps, embeddings into other spaces, or homeomorphisms to a compactified target. It cleanly separates an impossible target choice from questions about formulas.

The construction produces a valid finite homeomorphism, but not for the anticipated reason. Its displayed degree suggests a nonlinear pole map; reduction shows that it is the canonical normalized coordinate.

This has a negative and a constructive reading. The formula does not retain pole position and does not have minimal degree two. At the same time, the exact common factor responsible for degeneracy is now visible. A nontrivial tropical stereographic map must prevent the numerator and denominator from sharing all pole dependence after normalization.

The compactified result clarifies the formulation further. A statement about the finite quotient may be true while the corresponding endpoint-completed statement is impossible. Topological type must be fixed before degree, rationality, or conformal analogy is discussed.

More generally, tropical rational functions should be treated extensionally, as functions, rather than only intensionally, as displayed formulas. Syntactic data aid computation, but invariants must survive equivalent presentations.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{Higher-dimensional finite projective spaces.}
Define $\TP^n_{\mathrm{fin}}$ as the quotient of $\R^{n+1}$ by diagonal translation, choose a normalization, and test whether analogous pole expressions again collapse to affine maps. Several coordinate differences survive in higher dimensions, so cancellation may be partial.

\paragraph{Compactified codomains.}
The compactness obstruction shows that compactified $\TP^1$ cannot be homeomorphic to $\R$. A corrected target is $\overline{\R}$. Construct and classify homeomorphisms $\TP^1_{\mathrm{comp}}\to\overline{\R}$ directly from the quotient model and track endpoints.

\paragraph{Intrinsic tropical rational degree.}
Develop a representation-independent degree notion after cancellation of common tropical factors. Reduced breakpoint multiplicities and asymptotic slopes are natural candidates.

\paragraph{Pole degeneracy versus conformal information.}
Every finite pole yields the identity after normalization. Marked ends, metric graphs, harmonic morphisms, or incidence data may preserve pole position.

\paragraph{General tropical M\"obius maps.}
Classify one-dimensional differences of maxima of affine forms that are homeomorphisms. Determine whether every such slope-one homeomorphism is a translation after cancellation.

\paragraph{Max-plus and min-plus duality.}
Repeat the construction in the min-plus convention and establish equivalence under negation, including the reversal of compactified endpoints.

\section{Conclusion}

The inverse stereographic tropical lift on the finite tropical projective line is governed by
\[
\max(2x,x+p)-\max(x,p)=x.
\]
It follows that the map is a homeomorphism, every finite pole gives the same normalized transformation, and the quadratic-over-linear display does not represent minimal degree two. For the compactified line, no homeomorphism to the ordinary real line exists because compactness is preserved.

These results separate finite from compactified topology, displayed degree from intrinsic degree, and pole syntax from pole information. Any nontrivial higher-dimensional or enriched tropical stereography must be designed to survive all three tests.

